From b841a62fd1cf9136abb8f60d72b590881136bceb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 01:56:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/19] Measure seed coverage against published flights instead
 of inferring it

AFAC publishes flights per carrier in the VLOSREG sheet of the airline summary
workbook, which nothing else in the pipeline carries. With it the acceptance
test's central number stops being a model: sum the candidate's flights per
carrier, divide by the published count, normalise. The report now says which
path it took.

The measurement contradicts the inference it replaces. On the pilot the
inferred spread was 1.12x and the measured one is 1.30x, so the earlier reading
that the source comfortably passed does not hold. Two safeguards were needed to
get even that far: a carrier flying under a thousand flights a month is excluded
from the verdict, because Aerus flies 438 and the sampled Tuesday caught nine of
them, whose 0.64 alone stretched the spread to 1.74x; and a seed covering less
than half the month is flagged, because the spread then mixes coverage with
day-of-week mix. The pilot's pattern is exactly that shape -- Aeromexico 1.12
and Connect 1.06 high, Viva 0.86 low -- which is what a Tuesday does to a market
split between business and leisure carriers. Only a full month separates them.

Route flights exceed carrier flights by a steady 1.1%; the gap is the freight
airlines, which fly scheduled domestic legs and carry no passengers. Pinned by
a test so it is never mistaken for a parsing error.

